In the constructor of `BitDotProduct`, when converting a `List<Number>` to both byte and float arrays, the check for whether the numbers are floats is done after creating both arrays. This can be optimized by determining the type first and then only creating the necessary array:
```java
boolean isFloat = false;
for (int i = 0; i < queryVector.size(); i++) {
    Number number = queryVector.get(i);
    if (number.floatValue() % 1.0f != 0.0f || number.floatValue() < Byte.MIN_VALUE || number.floatValue() > Byte.MAX_VALUE) {
        isFloat = true;
        break;
    }
}
if (isFloat) {
    floatQueryVector = new float[queryVector.size()];
    for (int i = 0; i < queryVector.size(); i++) {
        floatQueryVector[i] = queryVector.get(i).floatValue();
    }
} else {
    byteQueryVector = new byte[queryVector.size()];
    for (int i = 0; i < queryVector.size(); i++) {
        byteQueryVector[i] = queryVector.get(i).byteValue();
    }
}
```

